            "[[cognitive-bias|Cognitive biases]] are predictable shortcuts in thinking. They are usually efficient and occasionally disastrous. Reviews of diagnostic error find that cognitive factors contribute to most errors, often alongside system problems, and that [[premature-closure|premature closure]] is the single most common cognitive cause.\n\nThe biases that matter most in neurology:\n\n- [[anchoring|Anchoring]]: locking onto the first impression and failing to adjust as new information arrives.\n- [[availability-bias|Availability]]: judging a diagnosis likely because it comes to mind easily, often after a recent or memorable case. It can cause overtesting as well as missed diagnoses.\n- [[premature-closure|Premature closure]]: accepting a diagnosis before it has been verified and stopping the search for alternatives.\n- [[diagnosis-momentum|Diagnosis momentum]]: a label gathers certainty as it is passed between clinicians and documents, until it is treated as established.\n- [[framing-effect|Framing]]: the way a problem is presented (\"known alcoholic,\" \"frequent attender,\" \"psychiatric patient, medically cleared\") steers what you look for.\n- [[confirmation-bias|Confirmation bias]]: seeking or weighting evidence that supports the current view and discounting evidence against it.\n\nThey cluster. In the hook, framing (\"intoxicated\") set the anchor, momentum carried it through three handovers, and premature closure meant no one examined for the cerebellar stroke that intoxication mimics.",
