            mechanism: "Fast, pattern-based thinking (recognizing a familiar picture) handles most clinical work well. Biases arise when a pattern is matched on a few features and the slower, analytic check is skipped. Fatigue, interruptions, crowding, and handovers make skipping more likely, which is why diagnostic errors cluster at night and at transitions of care.",
            compare: "Anchoring versus diagnosis momentum: anchoring happens inside one clinician's reasoning; momentum is social, passing between clinicians and records. Availability versus pretest probability: availability substitutes a vivid memory for the true frequency in this kind of patient, which the previous module taught you to estimate deliberately.",
            deeper: "Knowing the names of biases does not by itself prevent them. What helps is changing the routine: building in checkpoints where the analytic question must be asked (at handover, before discharge, when the course deviates) and making it acceptable for any team member to ask it. Slowing down when the stakes or the uncertainty are high is the practical form of thinking about your own thinking.",
